<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal</RootNamespace>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <Description>JSEAL — the JavaScript Engine Abstraction Layer. Engine-neutral contracts the HTML bridge binds the document against, so which JavaScript engine runs a page is a provider choice rather than a compile-time fact about the bridge.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.BroilerJs" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Broiler.HtmlBridge.Jseal.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Broiler.Browser.Core.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THIS PROJECT HAS NO ProjectReference AND NO PackageReference, AND THAT IS THE POINT.

    Engine neutrality is asserted everywhere else in this repository by grepping for a namespace.
    Here it is asserted by the compiler: an assembly that references nothing cannot name a
    Broiler.JavaScript type, a Broiler.VM type, or anything either of them drags in. If a future
    change needs one, the reference has to be added here first, in a diff a reviewer sees, rather
    than arriving transitively through a helper.

    scripts/check-engine-neutrality.sh fails the build if this element ever gains a child, so the
    property survives a refactor that did not read this comment.

    The corollary is that JSEAL cannot reach Broiler.HtmlBridge.Core either — no ContentSecurityPolicy,
    no MicroTaskQueue, no RenderLogger. That is deliberate and costs less than it looks: the policy
    decisions those types carry belong to the host, and what JSEAL needs from them is expressed as a
    capability (see JsCapabilities) rather than as a dependency. A provider is free to consult Core;
    the contracts are not.
  -->

</Project>
